## Video Script: AI Liability

Who is responsible when artificial intelligence causes harm?

Cases involving Air Canada, Tesla, and Uber show how difficult it can be to determine liability. Air Canada was held responsible for incorrect information provided by its chatbot, while the Tesla case raised questions about shared responsibility and remains subject to appeal. The Uber incident highlighted the importance of human supervision and safety.

In Canada, existing laws address AI-related harm, while the European Union has introduced updated product liability rules.

Ultimately, companies, developers, and human operators may share responsibility. As AI advances, clear laws and accountability are essential to protect people and ensure responsible innovation.
